    "title": "Postmortem Drug Redistribution: A Compilation of Postmortem/Antemortem Drug Concentration Ratios",
    "authors": [
      "Mantinieks D",
      "Gerostamoulos D",
      "Glowacki L",
      "Di Rago M",
      "Schumann J",
      "Woodford NW",
      "Drummer OH"
    ],
    "journal": "Journal of Analytical Toxicology",
    "year": 2021,
    "volume": "45",
    "issue": "4",
    "pages": "368-377",
    "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/jat/bkaa107"
  },
  "study": {
    "design": "Retrospective compilation of paired antemortem (AM) clinical specimens and postmortem (PM) mortuary-admission femoral blood from 811 coronial cases reported to the Victorian Coroner (Australia), 1 Jan 2009 - 31 Dec 2017.",
    "cases": 811,
    "pmMatrix": "Femoral whole blood taken by leg puncture at mortuary admission, preserved with 1% w/v sodium fluoride / potassium oxalate.",
    "amMatrix": "Routine clinical specimen taken closest to hospital admission - whole blood, plasma or serum. Plasma/serum accounted for about 35% of AM specimens.",
    "medianT1Hours": 31,
    "medianT2Hours": 17,
    "inclusionRule": "Only drugs with at least 10 paired AM/PM values are reported in Table I.",
    "statistics": "Related-Samples Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test or Related-Samples Sign Test, significance level 0.05.",
    "notReported": "Median PM/AM ratios for clonazepam (0.35) and nitrazepam (0.14) appear in the discussion as 'data not shown'. They are deliberately NOT seeded: the paper reports no n and no range for them, and its own inclusion rule means both had fewer than 10 paired cases, so pooling those medians as if they were Table I rows would overstate the evidence."
  },
  "caveats": [
    "A PM/AM ratio is a descriptive, population-level indicator of the change between a clinical specimen and mortuary-admission femoral blood. The paper's own conclusion is that there was no obvious relationship between AM and PM concentrations for most drugs, so a ratio must NOT be used to back-calculate an antemortem concentration from a measured postmortem one.",
    "Both time intervals vary widely between cases (t1 median 31 h, range 0-417; t2 median 17 h, range 3-135), and the ratio is time-dependent.",
    "About 35% of AM specimens were plasma or serum rather than whole blood, so part of the spread is blood:plasma distribution rather than postmortem change.",
    "Incomplete medical records meant drug administration by paramedics or hospital staff after the AM specimen could not be excluded - most relevant for morphine, diazepam and fentanyl.",
    "Some ratios below 1 reflect analyte instability or continued distribution rather than an absence of redistribution (olanzapine, risperidone/hydroxyrisperidone, quetiapine, diltiazem, nitrobenzodiazepines)."
  ],
